{"parentUuid":"5b38eb86-78ae-4092-8ef2-c7e5fba66341","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6fa20b73-a995-4ae4-b928-344e193a8ce4","agentId":"ac9e83ba97f6f4f5e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dr5i6F2ukKXqBBKMBTRBRe","type":"tool_result","content":"# Pumpkin-Solver-Py Directory Contents\n\nBased on the GitHub repository page, here's what exists in the pumpkin-solver-py directory:\n\n## (1) Tests Folder\nYes, there is a **tests/** folder present in the directory listing.\n\n## (2) Example Files\nYes, example files are available. The page specifically mentions that **examples can be run**, with \"nqueens\" referenced as a sample: `python examples/nqueens.py 5`. This indicates an **examples/** folder containing at least an nqueens example.\n\n## (3) Python Module Files\nThe page does not provide a detailed listing of specific Python module files within the src/ directory. While a **src/** folder is listed, the content shows only \"The python interface for the Pumpkin solver\" without enumerating individual modules for model, variables, constraints, optimization, or brancher functionality.\n\n**Summary:** The directory structure includes examples/, src/, and tests/ folders, with confirmed nqueens examples and pytest testing capability, but specific module file names aren't detailed in this view."}]},"uuid":"42c5fe51-3fdf-4b1c-babc-e7ffb53972d1","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:51:04.246Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5b38eb86-78ae-4092-8ef2-c7e5fba66341","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"64e8eae3-15e7-4632-951c-a05c9395ae0f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6fa20b73-a995-4ae4-b928-344e193a8ce4","agentId":"ac9e83ba97f6f4f5e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013XMzvZjwfrf6oZXEnQ3YeN","type":"tool_result","content":"# Glasgow Constraint Solver Analysis\n\nBased on the web page content provided:\n\n**(1) Total commit count:** 1,263 commits on the main branch.\n\n**(2) Most recent commit date:** Not explicitly stated in the provided content. The page shows commit history exists but doesn't display the specific date of the latest commit.\n\n**(3) Python files (gcspy.cc and gcspy.hh):** The content lists a `python/` directory in the repository structure, but does not specify whether these particular files exist within it.\n\n**(4) VeriPB 3.0 mention:** Yes. The documentation states: \"Currently we are using the VeriPB 3.0 format\" under the Proof Logging section.\n\n**(5) \"No stable API\" mention:** Yes, explicitly stated at the beginning: \"This solver is a work in progress, with no stable API or design.\"\n\n**(6) C++ standard and compiler requirements:** The documentation specifies \"A C++23 compiler\" is required. For minimum compiler version: \"GCC 13 (Ubuntu 24.04) is the oldest tested; GCC 15 and Clang 21 are the primary development compilers.\""}]},"uuid":"1da48931-407d-4ab9-bf40-db1faec3d3e1","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:51:04.932Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"64e8eae3-15e7-4632-951c-a05c9395ae0f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"8c4457ab-f9d0-4360-8dca-236ab21e2baf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6fa20b73-a995-4ae4-b928-344e193a8ce4","agentId":"ac9e83ba97f6f4f5e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XmH5CBgdLiWouVCihuMbSi","type":"tool_result","content":"# Huub Repository Analysis\n\n## (1) Does the repository exist?\n**Yes.** The repository `huub-solver/huub` exists on GitHub and is publicly accessible.\n\n## (2) Most recent commit date\n**Not visible in the provided content.** While the page indicates \"384 Commits\" on the develop branch, the specific dates of individual commits are not displayed in this excerpt.\n\n## (3) Is there Python binding?\n**Not evident.** The repository is \"Rust 98.1%\" according to the language breakdown shown. No mention of Python bindings appears in the available content.\n\n## (4) Files in crates/huub/src/constraints/ or similar\n**Directory structure not shown.** The folder tree visible displays:\n- `crates/` directory exists\n- `docs/`, `.github/`, `xtask/`, and `share/minizinc/huub/` folders are present\n\nHowever, the specific contents of `crates/huub/src/constraints/` are not detailed in this page view.\n\n## (5) CP 2025 paper mention\n**Not found.** The webpage content contains no reference to a CP 2025 (Constraint Programming 2025) paper or conference publication."}]},"uuid":"6b331241-b4b2-4cc6-b2e5-e25133895d2b","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:51:05.991Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8c4457ab-f9d0-4360-8dca-236ab21e2baf","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"bda1d4b7-8722-4b38-8027-6a9fd8fa4950","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6fa20b73-a995-4ae4-b928-344e193a8ce4","agentId":"ac9e83ba97f6f4f5e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XuU5LJJizwwyWSRMJ7Zavn","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG\" Dekker CP 2025 paper\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG Jip J. Dekker #\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG (Short Paper)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42\"},{\"title\":\"Google Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Towards+Modern+and+Modular+SAT+for+LCG+(Short+Paper).\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG\",\"url\":\"https://alexeyignatiev.github.io/assets/pdf/disz-cp25-preprint.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025)\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/volume/LIPIcs-volume-340\"},{\"title\":\"Satisfiability Modulo User Propagators | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/387475833_Satisfiability_Modulo_User_Propagators\"},{\"title\":\"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG (Short Paper). | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/search?q=Towards+Modern+and+Modular+SAT+for+LCG+(Short+Paper).\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - huub-solver/huub: CP+SAT solver framework built to be reliable, performant, and extensible · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/huub-solver/huub\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Effective Preprocessing in SAT Through Variable and Clause Elimination\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220944383_Effective_Preprocessing_in_SAT_Through_Variable_and_Clause_Elimination\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"}]\n\nI found the paper you're looking for. Here's the information:\n\n## Paper Details\n\nThe paper \"Towards Modern and Modular SAT for LCG\" was authored by Jip J. Dekker, Alexey Ignatiev, Peter J. Stuckey, and Allen Z. Zhong, and published in the 31st International Conference on Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming (CP 2025). It appears in Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics (LIPIcs), Volume 340, pp. 42:1-42:12, with DOI 10.4230/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nThe paper presents Huub, an LCG solver built based on the IPASIR-UP interface to SAT engines. This modular design allows swapping in any SAT solver that supports the interface, enabling the solver to benefit from the latest advancements in SAT solving technology.\n\nUsing the new IPASIR-UP interface to SAT engines, the authors construct an LCG solver which can swap out the underlying SAT engine with any that supports the interface. This new approach requires revisiting many of the design and engineering decisions for LCG solvers, and the paper explores the possibilities and challenges of using IPASIR-UP for LCG, showing that it can be used to create a highly competitive solver.\n\nThe paper is available at: https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol340-cp2025/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42/LIPIcs.CP.2025.42.pdf\n\nThe source code for Huub is available on GitHub: https://github.com/huub-solver/huub\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"76a719e7-aec8-41f7-8e3f-96d6bf572e2c","timestamp":"2026-05-09T23:51:10.252Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bda1d4b7-8722-4b38-8027-6a9fd8fa4950","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/zhuran24/claude-pj/zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.138","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
